<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD JATS (Z39.96) Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v1.0 20120330//EN" "JATS-archivearticle1.dtd"><article article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">Br. J. Cancer</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Nature Publishing Group</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">10188896</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2362720</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pii">6690238</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/sj.bjc.6690238</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Regular Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Serum YKL-40 and colorectal cancer</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Cintin</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Johansen</surname><given-names>J S</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Christensen</surname><given-names>I J</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff3">3</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Price</surname><given-names>P A</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff4">4</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>S&#x000f8;rensen</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff5">5</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Nielsen</surname><given-names>H J</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><aff id="aff1">Department of Surgical Gastroenterology, University of Copenhagen, Hvidovre Hospital, Copenhagen, Denmark</aff><aff id="aff2">Department of Rheumatology, University of Copenhagen, Hvidovre Hospital, Copenhagen, Denmark</aff><aff id="aff3">The Finsen Laboratory, University of Copenhagen, Rigshospitalet, Copenhagen, Denmark</aff><aff id="aff4">Department of Biology, University of California San Diego, La Jolla, California, USA</aff><aff id="aff5">Department of Clinical Biochemistry, University of Copenhagen, Hvidovre Hospital, Copenhagen, Denmark</aff></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>3</month><year>1999</year></pub-date><volume>79</volume><issue>9-10</issue><fpage>1494</fpage><lpage>1499</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>20</day><month>05</month><year>1998</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>30</day><month>09</month><year>1998</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>14</day><month>10</month><year>1998</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1999 Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-statement><copyright-year>1999</copyright-year><copyright-holder>Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-holder></permissions><abstract><p>YKL-40 is a mammalian member of the chitinase protein family. Although the function of YKL-40 is unknown, the pattern of its expression suggests a function in remodelling or degradation of extracellular matrix. High serum YKL-40 has been found in patients with recurrent breast cancer and has been related to short survival. In the present study we analysed YKL-40 in preoperative sera from patients with colorectal cancer and evaluated its relation to survival. Serum YKL-40 was determined by RIA in 603 patients. Survival after operation was registered, and median follow-up time was 61 months. Three hundred and forty patients died. Sixteen per cent of the patients with Dukes' A, 26&#x00025; with Dukes' B, 19&#x00025; with Dukes' C and 39&#x00025; with Dukes' D had high serum YKL-40 levels (adjusted for age). Analysis of serum YKL-40 as a continuous variable showed an association between increased serum YKL-40 and short survival (<italic>P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.0001). Patients with high preoperative serum YKL-40 concentration had significantly shorter survival than patients with normal YKL-40 (HR = 1.7; 95&#x00025; CI: 1.3&#x02013;2.1, <italic>P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.0001). Multivariate Cox analysis including serum YKL-40, serum CEA, Dukes' stage, age and gender showed that high YKL-40 was an independent prognostic variable for short survival (HR = 1.4; 95&#x00025; CI: 1.1&#x02013;1.8, <italic>P</italic> = 0.007). These results suggest that YKL-40 may play an important role in tumour invasion. &#x000a9; 1999 Cancer Research Campaign</p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>carcinoembryonic antigen</kwd><kwd>colorectal cancer</kwd><kwd>metastasis</kwd><kwd>tumour invasiveness</kwd><kwd>YKL-40/HC gp-39</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front></article>